00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:22.630
I do appreciate it.

00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:28.515
So I'm often asked to sort of
show how I learn, what do I do?

00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:29.505
How do I study?

00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:34.275
And I've often said that learning
vocabulary is, is the key.

00:00:34.275 --> 00:00:37.875
It's my number one goal:
increase my known words total.

00:00:38.235 --> 00:00:40.095
So how do I go about doing it?

00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:43.875
What is the role of flashcards
in my program of study?

00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:47.715
What is the role of, uh, you
know, spaced repetition systems?

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.900
So I'm going to demonstrate.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.020
I do most of my studying on my iPad.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.890
Some people do it on the, uh,
you know, on their computers.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.520
Some people use LingQ on
their iPhone or Android.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:03.900
I do it on a tablet.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.340
I do it on my iPad.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.330
So I'm going to show you that a little
later, but first a couple of principles.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:15.090
First of all, I believe that when we learn
words, we learn them within a holistic

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:21.030
sort of the whole language, it's part of
our becoming familiar with the language.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.700
The individual vocabulary items
are connected to the language.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.950
In a way they are not discreet.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.540
Now here's a word for those of you
who are non-native speakers, as

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:40.140
you know discreet D I S C R E E T
means to be, uh, you know, not to

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.430
be too obvious in what you're doing.

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:47.850
Uh, that's discreet, you know, not to
embarrass people or whatever, that's

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:54.360
discreet in that sense, but discrete, D I
S C R E T E means that it's not connected.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.050
It's separate.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.710
So vocabulary items are not discrete.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.130
They are connected.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.369
It's part of that holistic
getting used to the language.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.570
Until you are comfortable and
familiar with the language the

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.769
vocabulary items are not gonna stick.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.519
Or they'll stick for awhile
and then you'll forget them.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.700
So that's the second point
about vocabulary items.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.859
You're going to learn
them and forget them.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.290
And if you leave the language for awhile,
you're going to forget more of them, but

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:26.820
that's not a problem because once you
have learned them and then you go back in

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:31.500
there and you relearn them and rediscover
them or find them again, you do strengthen

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.390
your hold on those, because you see them
in more and more in different contexts.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:42.390
So it's important to learn
vocabulary in a meaningful content.

00:02:43.109 --> 00:02:47.280
So, uh, that's the third thing
about how I learned vocabulary.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.630
I, to the extent that I review
vocabulary items and I'll show you

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:57.210
that I do this more in lists than in
flashcards, I do it connected to content.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.960
So you'll see when I demonstrate that,
uh, every lesson that I'm in at LingQ,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.359
every content item, I will review the
vocabulary that's connected to that

00:03:06.359 --> 00:03:10.470
content item, because basically what I
want to do is get through the content

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.840
item, get through that item that I'm
studying and I'm looking at words and

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.910
listening to it's to, and where I listen
several times and still don't understand

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.010
the same parts or I look up words.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.630
And when I hear them, I can't
remember what they mean.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:29.420
And, and so it's all, uh, everything I do
is directed towards getting more meaning

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.080
out of the content without having to think
about it without having to translate.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:37.665
And so my review of vocabulary
is specific most of the time is

00:03:37.665 --> 00:03:41.924
specific to the content that I'm
studying, but there are exceptions.

00:03:42.775 --> 00:03:46.335
I like to feel good about
my language learning.

00:03:46.695 --> 00:03:49.454
And so I like to see more known words.

00:03:49.935 --> 00:03:56.204
And so, because typically I have
created a lot of LingQs, some of

00:03:56.204 --> 00:03:59.805
them I moved to status two, in other
words, I'm a little more familiar

00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:01.755
or status three, even more familiar.

00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:04.215
And I like to move them to known.

00:04:04.335 --> 00:04:09.585
So I will, and I'll demonstrate this as
well, I will go into my vocabulary section

00:04:10.215 --> 00:04:18.225
and then isolate the status three words
that I'm close to feeling I know, and I'll

00:04:18.225 --> 00:04:19.685
go in there and I'll move them to known.

00:04:20.345 --> 00:04:24.195
I move them to known, but I realize
that many of the words that I

00:04:24.195 --> 00:04:29.325
moved to known will slide to
status three or even status too.

00:04:29.415 --> 00:04:30.525
It doesn't bother me.

00:04:30.705 --> 00:04:35.475
I accept the fact that I'm going
to learn and forget and relearn.

00:04:36.195 --> 00:04:41.385
And the final thing I would say about
my approach to vocabulary acquisition,

00:04:41.985 --> 00:04:44.565
and use of flashcards and lists.

00:04:44.565 --> 00:04:48.045
And you'll see that I use lists
more than flashcards is that

00:04:48.045 --> 00:04:52.575
to me, reviewing vocabulary is
just another form of exposure.

00:04:53.265 --> 00:04:59.520
Uh, I don't believe that if I review
uh, you know, a bunch of items in an SRS

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.780
system, spaced repetition system that I'll
get some kind of a permanent hold on them.

00:05:03.989 --> 00:05:07.919
I still need them within a
lot of meaningful content.

00:05:08.489 --> 00:05:10.530
Uh, also, I don't believe...

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.510
I'm not a fan of flashcards because I
don't believe the time that I spend at

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:21.164
racking my brain trying to remember uh,
you know, if I see a word in, in either

00:05:21.164 --> 00:05:25.784
in my language or in the target language,
and I have to remember what that means,

00:05:26.295 --> 00:05:28.515
racking my brain to try and do that.

00:05:28.545 --> 00:05:29.805
I don't find that useful.

00:05:29.805 --> 00:05:30.914
I've never found that useful.

00:05:31.094 --> 00:05:35.235
So I go through the list as
another form of exposure.

00:05:35.625 --> 00:05:40.575
Uh, ultimately the language has got
to come in to your brain through

00:05:40.575 --> 00:05:45.195
exposure, reading content, listening
to content or reviewing lists are

00:05:45.195 --> 00:05:47.294
all different forms of exposure.

00:05:47.955 --> 00:05:51.615
Uh, so I don't like to rack
my brain using flashcards.

